l linuxacademy.py (added 2019):\n\n```python\nclass LinuxAcademyIE(InfoExtractor):\n    _VALID_URL = r'''(?x)\n        https?://\n            (?:\n                (?:www\\.)?linuxacademy\\.com/cp/modules/view/id-(?P<course_id>\\d+)\n                |\n                (?:api\\.)?(?:www\\.)?linuxacademy\\.com/cp/course/\n                (?:\n                    lessons/(?P<lesson_id>\\d+)|\n                    (?P<chapter_id>\\d+)\n                )\n            )\n    '''\n```\n\nHmm, I keep writing the same thing. Let me just check with get_info. The tool will tell me.\n\nActually, I recall the real one is:\n\n```python\n_VALID_URL = r'''(?x)\n    https?://\n        (?:\n            (?:www\\.)?linuxacademy\\.com/cp/modules/view/id-(?P<course_id>\\d+)\n            |\n            (?:api\\.)?(?:www\\.)?linuxacademy\\.com/cp/course/\n            (?:\n                lessons/(?P<lesson_id>\\d+)|\n                (?P<chapter_id>\\d+)\n            )\n        )\n'''\n```\n\nOK whatever, get_info will resolve it.\n\nNow the course webpage:\n\n```html\n<script>\nwindow.module = {\"title\": \"Course Title\", \"md_desc\": \"Course **markdown** description\", \"duration\": \"1:02:03\", \"items\": [...]};\n</script>\n```\n\nNote `_search_regex(r'window\\.module\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;', webpage, 'module')` \u2014 the regex is non-greedy `{.+?}` followed by `\\s*;`. So the JSON must be followed by `;`. If the JSON contains `}` followed later by `;`... non-greedy will match up to the first `}` followed by optional whitespace and `;`. So JSON must not contain `};` inside... Actually `{.+?}` non-greedy: it tries the shortest match where after `}` comes `\\s*;`. If JSON contains nested objects like `{\"a\": {\"b\": 1}};` \u2014 the first `}` is followed by `}` not `;`, so it continues. The final `};` matches. But if a nested object is followed by `;`... unlikely in JSON. But if JSON contains a string with `};`... avoid that.\n\nAlso note: `.+?` doesn't match newlines by default (no DOTALL). So the JSON must be on a single line. I'll write the JSON compactly on one line.\n\nItems for the course path:\n\n1. A non-dict item: `\"not-a-dict\"` (string) \u2192 line 172-173 continue.\n2. A section item: `{\"type\": {\"name\": \"Section\", \"slug\": \"section\"}, \"course_name\": \"Chapter 1\", \"course_module\": \"module-1\"}` \u2192 lines 180-182. chapter = \"Chapter 1\", chapter_id = \"module-1\", chapter_number = 1.\n3. Another section to bump chapter_number to 2: `{\"type\": {\"slug\": \"section\"}, \"course_name\": \"Chapter 2\", \"course_module\": \"module-2\"}` \u2192 chapter_number = 2. This covers `1 if not chapter_number else chapter_number + 1` both branches.\n4. A non-lesson item: `{\"type\": {\"name\": \"Quiz\"}}` \u2192 line 186 continue.\n5. A lesson item without url: `{\"type\": {\"name\": \"lesson\"}}` \u2192 line 189 continue. Wait, `urljoin(url, item.get('url'))` \u2014 if `item.get('url')` is None, `urljoin(url, None)` returns None. Yes, urljoin returns None if the second arg is None. Actually `urljoin(base, url)`: if url is None \u2192 returns None? Let me check: `urljoin('http://a/', None)` \u2014 in Python, `urljoin` with None as url raises AttributeError? Let me think. `urllib.parse.urljoin(b, u)`: it does `if not u: return b`? No wait:\n\n```python\ndef urljoin(base, url, allow_fragments=True):\n    if not base:\n        return url\n    if not url:\n        return base\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually Python's urljoin:\n\n```python\ndef urljoin(base, url, allow_fragments=True):\n    if not base:\n        return url\n    if not url:\n        return base\n    bscheme, bnetloc, bpath, bparams, bquery, bfragment = urlparse(base, '', allow_fragments)\n    ...\n```\n\nWait, but youtube_dl.utils.urljoin is:\n\n```python\ndef urljoin(base, path):\n    if isinstance(path, bytes):\n        path = path.decode('utf-8')\n    if not isinstance(path, compat_str) or not path:\n        return None\n    return compat_urllib_parse_urljoin(base, path)\