test. Good \u2014 no state pollution.\n\nAlternatively use `pytest-mock`'s `mocker`. The instructions say use monkeypatch or pytest-mock if appropriate. I'll use monkeypatch (no extra dependency).\n\nNow, about `_download_webpage` fake: signature in real code is `_download_webpage(self, url, video_id, note=None, errnote=None, fatal=True, data=None, headers=None, query=None)`. The call is `self._download_webpage(url, item_id)`. My fake: `def fake(self, url, video_id, *args, **kwargs)`. Good.\n\n`_extract_m3u8_formats` call: `self._extract_m3u8_formats(m3u8_url, item_id, 'mp4', entry_protocol='m3u8_native', m3u8_id='hls')`. Fake: `def fake(self, m3u8_url, video_id, ext=None, *args, **kwargs)`. I'll capture kwargs to assert entry_protocol='m3u8_native' and m3u8_id='hls' and ext='mp4'. Good postcondition assertions.\n\nReturn formats: `[{'url': m3u8_url, 'ext': 'mp4', 'format_id': 'hls-1', 'protocol': 'm3u8_native'}]`. Then `_sort_formats(formats)` sorts in place; info['formats'] is the same list. Assert `info['formats'][0]['url'] == m3u8_url`.\n\nNow let me write the course webpage. Important: the regex `window\\.module\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;` \u2014 I'll write:\n\n```html\n<html><body><script>window.module = {\"title\":\"Course Title\",\"md_desc\":\"Course description\",\"duration\":\"1:02:03\",\"items\":[...]};</script></body></html>\n```\n\nAll on one line to satisfy `.+?` (no DOTALL). Actually `.` matches everything except newline; if the JSON is on one line, fine.\n\nWait, one more consideration: `_search_regex` with `({.+?})` \u2014 group 1 is the JSON. `_parse_json` then parses it. The JSON must be valid. Note: `{.+?}` non-greedy \u2014 it will match the shortest string ending with `}` such that the rest matches `\\s*;`. Since JSON ends with `}` followed by `;`, and inner `}`s are followed by `,` or `}` etc., the match will be the full JSON. But careful: if any string value inside contains `};`... avoid. Also if an inner object is the last thing before the closing